# WINARM (P5, sprint_p05_detail.md D5 row 2): the win_io twin of the POSIX
# file-I/O slice — UCRT _open/_creat/_read/_write/_close via the resolver's
# underscore alias. Loads everywhere (FFI pragmas resolve at call time);
# RUNS only on Windows (the platformName guard in 99_run_all.mst).
59_win_io_tests.mst
# WINARM (WG0, docs/sprints/sprint_wg0_detail.md): the Windows GUI world
# LAYER, loaded from here — the one place outside world/winui.list that
# names it, and not by choice. The probe classes deliberately stay out of
# world.list (the base world must remain byte-identical), but an unresolved
# capitalised name in a method body is a COMPILE error in this dialect and
# the guest has no dynamic `Smalltalk at:` to reach a global with, so
# 60_winui_probe_tests.mst cannot load at all unless its layer loaded
# first. `..` resolves against world/tests/, i.e. world/90_winui_probe.mst;
# both `it_world.rs::load_tests_list` and the justfile's concatenating
# differential gates handle it identically. The layer is inert off Windows
# (FFI pragmas resolve at CALL time) and its tests are platformName-guarded
# in 99_run_all.mst.
../90_winui_probe.mst
# WINARM (WG1, docs/sprints/sprint_wg1_detail.md): the second half of the
# same layer — WinArena, WinApi, WinShell. Loaded here for the identical
# compile-time reason and in winui.list's own order (90 forward-declares
# WinArena/WinApi with empty bodies; 91 reopens them and names WinRef back).
# Nothing in either file executes at load time, which is what makes that
# mutual reference safe rather than clever.
../91_winui_shell.mst
# WINARM (WG3, docs/sprints/sprint_wg3_detail.md): the third layer file —
# WinLayout and WinControl, and the four WinShell hooks 91 declares as no-ops.
# Loaded here for the identical compile-time reason and in winui.list's own
# order. WG2's note below said "no third layer file"; WG3 has one, and the
# thing that made WG1's Δ 13 fire was a gate recipe NAMING a layer file
# directly rather than the layer growing — both `just gate-wg3` and this list
# read winui.list, so a fourth cannot break either.
../92_winui_controls.mst
60_winui_probe_tests.mst
61_winui_shell_tests.mst
# WINARM (WG2, docs/sprints/sprint_wg2_detail.md): the WndProc door's
# in-language half — the dispatcher's two-sided allowlist, its LRESULT
# contract, and the address channel (primitive 272). No third layer file:
# WG2 extended 91 rather than adding 92, so `winui.list` is unchanged and
# WG1's Δ 13 (a gate recipe that named one layer file directly) cannot fire
# again from here.
62_winui_door_tests.mst
# WINARM (WG3): the flag-and-drain pass and the layout object, in-language.
63_winui_controls_tests.mst
# WINARM (WG4): the shell's layout grammar and view registry, in-language.
../93_winui_shell.mst
# WG4 D3/D4/D5 add the drawing, focus and splitter layers, and 64's pure
# tests reach into all three (the glyph table, the signed-word split, the
# dock clamp) — so they load here for the same reason ../90..93 do.
../94_winui_drawing.mst
../95_winui_focus.mst
../96_winui_splitter.mst
../97_winui_workspace.mst
64_winui_shell_tests.mst
# WINARM (WG5b): the Browser and the host service. 98 is loaded here for the
# same compile-time reason the rest are - 65 names WinShell methods it
# defines - and 99 must follow it, since its `acceptSource` reaches the
# browser's own selection accessors.
../98_winui_browser.mst
../99_winui_host.mst
65_winui_host_tests.mst
99_run_all.mst
